\title{Visualizing divisibility}
\date{2026-07-11T17:49:11Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{We can illustrate the situation as follows:}

\diagram{200}{*}{\verb<<<|
dia = frame 1 . vsep 2 $
  [ hsep 3 . map centerY $
    [ vsep 1 . map center $
      [ mconcat
        [ text "b" <> rect 1 0.1 # lw none # fc white
        , hrule 9
        ]
      , hcat (replicate 3 a)
      ]
    ]

  , hsep 3 . map centerY $
    [ vsep 1 . map center $
      [ mconcat
        [ text "b" <> rect 1 0.1 # lw none # fc white
        , hrule 10
        ]
      , hcat (replicate 3 a ++ [r])
      ]
    ]
  ]
  where
    a = text "a" <> rect 3 1 # fc lightblue
    r = text "r" <> rect 1 1 # fc lightgreen
<<<}

\p{In the situation illustrated at the top, #{b} can be covered exactly with three copies of #{a}, so #{a \mid b}. On the other hand, in the bottom illustration, attempting to cover #{b} with copies of #{a} leaves a bit left over; hence #{a \nmid b}. Thinking about pictures like this can help us build the right intuition. However, keep in mind that the formal definition is a bit more general than these pictures, since the formal definition allows #{a} and #{b} to be negative, or zero, which the pictures can’t really show.}

